DASH.todo={title:"What to do as a normal investor",note:"General info, not personal financial advice.",
l:["<b>Rates are the story — plan for higher, not lower.</b> The 10Y jumped back above 5.1% and a December Fed hike is ~85% priced; avoid new floating-rate or high-interest debt and keep an emergency cash buffer.",
"<b>Don't let one CEO headline drive your money.</b> Nvidia lost its CEO a week before earnings (17 Nov); a broad or equal-weight fund spreads single-stock risk better than chasing AMD/Broadcom or panic-selling Nvidia.",
"<b>Keep investing regularly, not all at once.</b> Fear & Greed is 40 (Fear) and new lows swamped new highs (222 vs 39), so steady dollar-cost averaging into broad funds beats trying to time the bottom.",
"<b>Cash and short bonds pay you to wait.</b> With the 2Y near 4.87% and more hikes priced, short-term deposits or bond funds are a fair place for money you need within a year or two.",
"<b>Fuel relief is fragile.</b> Brent jumped to $95.82 after Trump said no Iran deal is needed; diesel is still ~$6.10, so don't budget for cheaper fuel yet.",
"<b>Gold is a hedge, not a rocket.</b> It fell to a one-year low near $3,880 as yields and the dollar rose; keep it a small slice and don't catch the falling knife with borrowed money.",
"<b>Keep crypto small.</b> BTC slid ~3% to ~$71.4k on risk-off and rate fears; only hold what you can leave alone.",
"<b>Thai investors: foreigners are buying again.</b> The SET held ~1,499 while foreigners bought ฿1.71B; brokers favor selective buying in names with strong Q3 results (HANA, CPALL, CRC). In our dividend table, SPALI, ICHI, PTTEP, AP, TVO and PTT still trade at or below their buy zones (green) — check payout safety first.",
"<b>War headlines swing both ways.</b> Iran talks were shut down and Putin hardened his line in one day; don't bet big on a single peace or war headline."]};
// Glossary: m = regex matched against an element's text (li/td/h2); d = plain explanation + what it means now.
DASH.glossary={
fg:{m:"Fear & Greed",d:"CNN's 0-100 mood gauge for US stocks, built from 7 signals. 40 = Fear, up from 37 Wednesday and well above 20 a week ago. Momentum is still extreme fear; put/call and safe-haven demand are fear; junk bonds read greed."},
fgbands:{d:"0-25 Extreme Fear, 26-45 Fear, 46-55 Neutral, 56-75 Greed, 76-100 Extreme Greed. Fear often comes before rebounds, but not always."},
nk:{m:"Nuclear Escalation",d:"Our editorial 0-100 tension score from weighted news factors. Not a probability. ~46 = Elevated (up from ~43): Trump shut the Iran talks channel, tankers were hit in Hormuz, Houthis hit Saudi oil sites and Putin hardened his Ukraine line."},
nkbands:{d:"0-20 Low, 21-40 Guarded, 41-60 Elevated, 61-80 High, 81-100 Severe. Each factor's score × weight adds up to the total."},
mom:{m:"Market momentum",d:"S&P 500 vs its 125-day average. 16 = extreme fear: the index is still below its recent trend after Thursday's 0.35% dip."},
str:{m:"price strength",d:"Stocks at 52-week highs vs lows. 48.8 = neutral on CNN's measure, but on the NYSE lows swamped highs Thursday (39 highs vs 222 lows)."},
brd:{m:"breadth",d:"Volume in rising vs falling stocks. 51.2 = neutral, slipping as small caps (Russell 2000 −1.66%) lagged Thursday."},
pc:{m:"Put/Call",d:"Compares bets on stocks falling vs rising. 26.2 = fear, improved from extreme fear as Nvidia's drop was absorbed."},
vix:{m:"VIX",d:"The market's 'fear gauge' of expected S&P swings. Reading 50 = neutral; the VIX itself rose 6.9% to 18.70 Thursday."},
sh:{m:"Safe-haven",d:"Stock returns vs bonds lately. 29.2 = fear, improving: bonds sold off hard, so stocks look relatively better."},
jb:{m:"Junk bond",d:"Extra yield on risky company debt. 58 = greed: junk-bond demand is holding up despite the rate jump."},
f1:{m:"Active wars",d:"Wars that could pull in nuclear powers. Scored 61 (from 57): Trump said the US doesn't need a deal and shut Vance's Iran channel; Iran keeps its enriched uranium. Iran isn't a confirmed nuclear state."},
f2:{m:"Russia-Ukraine",d:"A nuclear power at war. Scored 39 (from 37): Putin told US envoys he won't compromise on annexed regions and called foreign peacekeepers a legitimate target; talks continue."},
f3:{m:"spillover",d:"Risk the Mideast war widens. Scored 78 (from 75): Iran said four tankers were hit in Hormuz and Houthis claimed attacks on Saudi oil sites."},
f4:{m:"diplomacy channels",d:"Are big powers talking? Higher = fewer working channels. Scored 15 (from 12): the US-Iran channel was shut, though the US-Russia-Ukraine track continues."},
f5:{m:"Arms-control",d:"Nuclear threats and treaty news. Scored 33 (from 32): no explicit nuclear threats, but Iran refuses to ship out enriched uranium and Putin warned foreign troops would be targets."},
sp:{m:"S&P",d:"500 big US companies, the main US stock benchmark. ~7,592 Thursday (−0.35%) as Nvidia's CEO exit was offset by AMD and Broadcom; 2.9% below the 6 Oct record."},
dow:{m:"Dow",d:"30 large 'blue-chip' US stocks, more old-economy. −0.36% Thursday to about 52,297."},
ndq:{m:"Nasdaq",d:"Tech-heavy US index. −0.39% Thursday to about 25,366; Nvidia's earnings on 17 Nov are the next test."},
y10:{m:"10Y",d:"Yield on 10-year US government bonds, which drives loan rates. ~5.10% Thursday (+11.6bp), the highest since 21 Oct, as bonds reopened after the holiday."},
wti:{m:"WTI",d:"US crude oil price. ~$88.7 (+2.6%) Thursday after Trump said no Iran deal is needed."},
brent:{m:"Brent",d:"Global benchmark crude. ~$95.8 (+3.3%), briefly above $96; still ~2.5% lower on the week."},
dsl:{m:"diesel",d:"US average diesel price. ~$6.10/gal (AAA, 12 Nov), easing slowly but still above $6; the 22 Sep record was $6.53."},
gold:{m:"^Gold$|Gold and|gold slice",d:"Classic safe haven. ~$3,880 (−2.8%), its lowest since October 2025, as yields and the dollar rose; support ~$3,850, resistance ~$3,900-3,944."},
btc:{m:"Bitcoin|BTC",d:"The largest crypto. ~$71.4k = bearish: −3% on risk-off after Nvidia's CEO exit and higher yields; $70k is the line to watch."},
bull:{d:"BULLISH = we expect it to rise or hold strong. MILDLY = a leaning, not a strong view."},
neu:{d:"NEUTRAL = no clear direction; it could go either way."},
bear:{d:"BEARISH = we expect it to fall or stay weak."},
ff:{m:"Fed funds",d:"The Fed's key short-term interest rate. 3.75-4.00% after September's hike, held on 28 Oct; next decision 8-9 Dec, where a hike is ~85% priced."},
cpi:{m:"CPI",d:"Consumer price inflation. October rose 0.4% MoM (est. 0.3%) and core 0.3% (est. 0.2%), a third straight upside surprise; no official YoY because of last year's data gap, but the Cleveland Fed estimates ~3.8%, well above the Fed's 2% goal."},
ppi:{m:"PPI",d:"Prices businesses pay, an early inflation signal. October PPI is due Fri 13 Nov (8:30pm Bangkok); forecasts are +6.6% YoY headline and +5.0% core."},
odds:{m:"Hike odds|Fed odds",d:"Market-implied chance of a Fed hike (fed funds futures, CME FedWatch). ~85% for a quarter point on 9 Dec and ~15% for a half point; about three hikes priced by March."},
dxy:{m:"Dollar index",d:"DXY: the dollar against 6 major currencies. ~103.2, near a 19-month high; the euro is ~$1.105."},
jpy:{m:"USD/JPY|Yen",d:"Yen per dollar. Weakened past 160 for the first time since September as US yields jumped — the level where Japan has warned about intervention."},
cds:{m:"CDS",d:"Credit default swap: insurance against a company defaulting. CoreWeave's CDS went past 11% (a distress level) in October; its raised outlook and 2028 profit target sent the stock up 24.7% on 11 Nov."},
bot:{m:"BOT policy|BoT meeting|BOT MPC|BoT decision|BoT held|BoT kept",d:"Bank of Thailand's key rate. Held at 1.00% on 28 Oct (unanimous). Minutes released 11 Nov say policy should stay accommodative, but low rates amid global tightening add financial-stability risk; next decision 23 Dec."},
set:{m:"SET",d:"SET = Thai stock index; SET50 = its 50 biggest stocks. 1,499.06 Thu 12 Nov (−0.20%), hovering at 1,500; brokers see support ~1,490 and resistance 1,515-1,522."},
flow:{m:"Foreign flows|foreigners",d:"Net buying minus selling by foreign investors. +฿1,711.12M on 12 Nov (after −฿713M on 11 Nov); local institutions −฿1,064M, Thai retail −฿139M (SET via InfoQuest)."},
dca:{m:"DCA",d:"Dollar-cost averaging: invest a fixed amount on a schedule regardless of price, which smooths out bad timing."}};

